# The same program as examples/pipeline.vine, not one character changed, over
# a second day's log off the same runner. The second file is where a program
# finds out what it did not know, and this one was written from what such a
# runner really does between one day and the next, not from a list of cases
# the program might have.
#
# Four things differ, and two of them cost nothing:
#
#   - The build ids are five digits and no longer fit the first log's column,
#     and there is a step -- `publish` -- the first log never had. Both are
#     free: every width in the report is measured and every step name is a
#     map key.
#   - The log has been rotated, so it begins in the middle of a build whose
#     `start` lines are in yesterday's file. Line 1 is an `ok` with no start,
#     and the program says so. That is the right answer and the only one it
#     can give.
#   - build-10232 runs its tests as a matrix: two shards, at once, under one
#     step name. The runner emits two `start` lines and two `ok` lines and
#     nothing in them says which end belongs to which start. The program
#     pairs (build, step), so it reports one step started twice and one end
#     with no start, and counts one test run of the two. Both questions are
#     false and the program is not wrong: the log does not carry the fact it
#     would need. This is the difference no second file could have been
#     designed to produce and none of the first log's seven questions
#     resembles.
examples/pipeline.vine
